---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE, PORTFOLIO } from '../../config';
---
<Base lang="en" title="Privacy policy" description="How this site collects and uses your information.">
  <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
  <p class="meta">Last updated: 25 November 2026</p>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>This is the games site of <strong>{SITE.author}</strong>, made for fun. There are no accounts, no logins and no real money.</p>

    <h2>Information stored on your device</h2>
    <p>High scores, game progress and your light or dark theme choice are stored in your own browser (localStorage) so the site can remember them next time. This data is not sent anywhere and you can remove it by clearing the site data in your browser.</p>

    <h2>Analytics</h2>
    <p>To learn how many people visit and which games they play, the site uses <strong>Google Analytics 4</strong> from Google. It uses cookies to tell visits apart and records anonymous figures such as pages viewed, country, device type and traffic source. Google's advertising and audience features are turned off, the figures are not used for advertising and they are not sold. You can block cookies in your browser or use a tracking blocker if you prefer not to be counted.</p>

    <h2>Online rooms (Tien Len, Poker, Xi Dach, Gomoku, Chess and Xiangqi)</h2>
    <p>When you create or join an online room, the devices exchange data through <strong>Google's Firebase Realtime Database</strong> (server in Singapore). The site signs your device in anonymously (no email or password), so Google issues a random ID and sees your network (IP) address, as any web service does. The data sent includes the room code, the name you chose, your actions in the game (playing cards, passing, ready; in poker: check, call, raise, fold; moves in the board games), the chat messages and reactions you send in a room, and the game state, including your cards. Chat only goes to people in the room and is not saved, so do not send private information. Security rules let only the recipient read messages addressed to them; messages are deleted as soon as they are read and room entries are removed when you leave. The room host runs the rules of the game, so in theory the host can see everyone's cards; play with friends. As the owner of the Firebase project I can technically view data while it is on the server, but I do not use or keep it. Your browser only remembers your name and a reconnect code in localStorage so you can rejoin after reloading the page (for up to 30 minutes), and you can remove it by leaving the room or clearing the site data. Google processes the data under the <a href="https://firebase.google.com/support/privacy" rel="noopener">Firebase privacy policy</a>.</p>

    <h2>Hosting</h2>
    <p>The site is hosted on GitHub Pages. As with any web host, the provider may record basic technical details of visits, such as IP addresses, under its own policy.</p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>For any privacy question, see the contact details on <a href={`${PORTFOLIO}/en/contact/`} rel="noopener">Khang's portfolio</a>.</p>

    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>If the site changes how it collects information, I will update this page and the date at the top.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
